[test-suite name=llm-antipatterns ring=1
  [doc [#
# LLM anti-pattern fixture pairs (#938)

The executable backing for the ANTI-PATTERNS section of the generated LLM
primer (`docs/llm/primer.md`, emitted by `scripts/gen_docs/primer_build.cx`).

Each entry in that section is a PAIR: a `…-wrong` case carrying the form an
assistant reaches for out of Lisp/Clojure/shell habit, and a `…-right` case
carrying the CX form beside it. Both halves execute. The primer never states
what the wrong form does — it shows the recorded output, and the drift gate
(`make docs-check`) fails if the shipped binary stops producing it.

## Why this suite is not `conformance/code.cxd`

Two reasons, both about the CONTRACT of the recorded output.

  1. `code.cxd`'s `[out-err]` records an ERROR CODE (`cx-err:CXER0271`),
     deliberately: the conformance corpus pins semantics, not wording, so a
     message rewrite is not a conformance break. The primer teaches the
     OPPOSITE thing — the reader must recognise the diagnostic they will
     actually see on their terminal. So this suite records the message
     BYTE-EXACT, and a message rewrite IS a break here (regenerate the
     primer in the same commit; that is the whole point of the gate).

  2. Half of these anti-patterns are CLI-INVOCATION errors, not language
     errors — flag placement, `cx eval` misuse. They have no expression in a
     fixture schema whose only inputs are a document and a program. This
     suite adds `[cli-argv]` for them.

It lives under `conformance/llm/` rather than `conformance/*.cxd` so the
top-level corpus scanners (`vcx/tests/cxparse_full_corpus_diff_test.v`,
`child_scan_differential_test.v` — both `os.ls` of the conformance root)
keep their pinned baselines: this suite's contract is not theirs.

## Format

Standard `[case id=… level=…]` with `[in-cx]` / `[in-code]`, plus:

| Section | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[title]` | one-line label; the primer renders it as the pair's heading |
| `[in-cx]` | input document; `[empty]` means "no document input" |
| `[in-code]` | the program |
| `[cli-argv]` | ONE argv token per line. Placeholders: `%FILE%` = the path the `[in-code]` body was written to, `%DATA%` = the path the `[in-cx]` body was written to, `%CODE%` = the `[in-code]` body itself as a single argv token. Absent = `--data=%DATA% %FILE%`, or just `%FILE%` when `[in-cx]` is `[empty]`. |
| `[out-text]` | byte-exact expected output: stdout when stdout is non-empty, else stderr (edge newlines normalised) |
| `[exit-code]` | expected process exit code |
| `[why]` | the prose the primer prints under the pair |

## Runner

`scripts/gen_docs/primer_build.cx` executes every case referenced by a
template — here and in every other suite the primer draws from — and

  * FAILS if live output != `[out-text]` (or live rc != `[exit-code]`),
  * substitutes the LIVE output into the generated Markdown.

`make docs-check` runs it without writing and additionally requires the
regenerated bytes to equal the committed `docs/llm/`. That gate is in
`TEST_TARGETS` and carries a `tools/release-verify.sh` row.
#]]

 [; ── A1. cx flags after the FILE ─────────────────────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-flags-after-file-wrong level=core
  [title [#cx flags placed after the file#]]
  [tags cli flags capability antipattern 938]
  [why [#
The run surface is `cx [cx-flags] FILE [program-args...]` — the interpreter
convention. Everything AFTER the file is the PROGRAM's argv (#926), so
`--allow-env` there is a string handed to the program, not a grant handed to
`cx`. The capability is never granted and the program dies at its first
effect. Nothing warns you: the flag was consumed, just not by cx.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env' as=env]
[probe args=[$count [$env:argv]] home=[$env:var-or-default 'CX_PRIMER_ABSENT' 'unset']]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
%FILE%
--allow-env
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: env capability required for env-var-or-default; none granted (grant via --allow-env)']
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-flags-after-file-right level=core
  [title [#cx flags placed before the file#]]
  [tags cli flags capability 938]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env' as=env]
[probe args=[$count [$env:argv]] home=[$env:var-or-default 'CX_PRIMER_ABSENT' 'unset']]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
--allow-env
%FILE%
#]]
  [out-text [#
[probe args=1 home=unset]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A2. bare head name vs the $ call sigil ───────────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-bare-head-typo-wrong level=core
  [title [#A bare head name silently becomes DATA#]]
  [tags call construction sigil typo antipattern 938]
  [why [#
CX is homoiconic: `[name …]` is a legal ELEMENT, so a bare head that resolves
to nothing is not an error — it is a document. A misspelled call therefore
succeeds, exit 0, and hands the rest of the program a `[greeet 'world']`
element where a string was expected. The `$` sigil is the assertion "this is
a call": with it, the same typo fails loud and names itself.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def greet ($who) [$concat "hello " $who]]
[greeet "world"]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[greeet 'world']
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-bare-head-typo-right level=core
  [title [#The $ call sigil fails loud on the same typo — and names the candidate#]]
  [tags call sigil typo did-you-mean 938 1058]
  [why [#
The `$` sigil turns the typo into a refusal, and since #1058 T1.5 the refusal
also names the CANDIDATE: the def table is the closed set the resolver just
searched, so `greeet` is one edit from `greet` and the message says so. The
suggestion is deliberately conservative — a name far from everything gets none,
because a wrong suggestion sends the reader to check a name they never wrote.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def greet ($who) [$concat "hello " $who]]
[$greeet "world"]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0136 message='E_NO_CALLABLE: no callable "greeet" — did you mean `greet`?']
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [; ── A3. nested [?let] vs flat multi-binding ──────────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-nested-let-wrong level=core
  [title [#Nested [?let] (the Scheme let* habit)#]]
  [tags let multi-binding antipattern 938]
  [why [#
`[?let]` is ALREADY sequential — each `[= $name value]` sees every binding
before it (EV-LET-SEQ). Nesting adds a scope level per binding and buys
nothing: the two forms below are output-identical. Flat is the house form;
three nested `[?let]`s is a rewrite an assistant should never emit.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $a 1]
  [?let [= $b [+ $a 1]]
    [?let [= $c [+ $b 1]]
      [row a=$a b=$b c=$c]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[row a=1 b=2 c=3]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-nested-let-right level=core
  [title [#Flat multi-binding [?let]#]]
  [tags let multi-binding 938]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $a 1] [= $b [+ $a 1]] [= $c [+ $b 1]]
  [row a=$a b=$b c=$c]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[row a=1 b=2 c=3]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A4. wide [?match] in a per-node hot path ─────────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-wide-match-hot-path-wrong level=core
  [title [#A wide [?match] on a per-node hot path#]]
  [tags match if dispatch performance antipattern 938]
  [why [#
`[?match]` is a pattern-matching dispatch, not a switch statement, and what
an arm COSTS depends on what its pattern binds. Bind-free arms — scalar
literals, the wildcard — are at PARITY with the equivalent flat
`[?if]`/`[=]` chain (measured ~0.96x on a 40-arm literal table), and a
structural single-arm destructure runs at 0.94-0.98x of a hand-written flat
bind chain, so neither is worth rewriting for speed. Arms carrying a BINDING
pattern are the remaining hot-path shape: an element pattern, a `$bind`, a
collection pattern each pay a per-arm environment rollback whose cost scales
with the PROGRAM (how much is in scope), not with the pattern — so a wide
element-pattern dispatch run once per node of a document gets dramatically
more expensive as the program grows around it, which is the trap. The rule:
reach for `[?match]` to take a value APART; on a per-node branch over element
shapes, dispatch on the head name with a flat `[?if]` chain. The pair below
proves that rewrite is output-preserving. (Historic note for anyone holding
older numbers: the "~500x / ~20-45us per arm" figure this entry used to quote
was retired by the dispatch work in cx-private #850, #1094 and #1139 — it no
longer describes any shape.)
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def kind-of ($n)
  [?match $n
    [case [leaf] 'leaf']
    [case [twig] 'twig']
    [case [branch] 'branch']
    [case [trunk] 'trunk']
    [else 'other']]]
[?for [in $n ([leaf], [twig], [branch], [trunk], [root])] [yield [$kind-of $n]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
leaf
twig
branch
trunk
other
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-wide-match-hot-path-right level=core
  [title [#A flat [?if] chain on the same dispatch#]]
  [tags match if dispatch performance 938]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def kind-of ($n)
  [?let [= $t [$name $n]]
    [?if [= $t 'leaf']   [then 'leaf']
    [else [?if [= $t 'twig']   [then 'twig']
    [else [?if [= $t 'branch'] [then 'branch']
    [else [?if [= $t 'trunk']  [then 'trunk']
    [else 'other']]]]]]]]]]
[?for [in $n ([leaf], [twig], [branch], [trunk], [root])] [yield [$kind-of $n]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
leaf
twig
branch
trunk
other
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A5. `cx eval` with an inline program ─────────────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-cx-eval-inline-wrong level=core
  [title [#`cx eval 'PROGRAM'` — the subcommand takes a FILE#]]
  [tags cli eval inline antipattern 938]
  [why [#
`cx eval` is a legacy ALIAS of the default run action and, like it, takes a
FILE. An inline program needs `-e`. The habit of writing
`cx eval '<source>'` (from other interpreters) makes cx try to OPEN the
program text as a path. Two rules avoid the whole class: run files with
`cx FILE`, and reach for `cx -e 'PROGRAM'` only for a one-liner.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[+ 1 2]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
eval
%CODE%
#]]
  [out-text [#
cx eval: error reading program "[+ 1 2]": failed to open file "[+ 1 2]"; code: 2
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-cx-eval-inline-right level=core
  [title [#`cx -e 'PROGRAM'` for a one-liner#]]
  [tags cli inline expression 938]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[+ 1 2]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
-e
%CODE%
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A6. a bare fraction is a DECIMAL, and decimals do not mix with floats ── ]

 [case id=ap-decimal-float-mix-wrong level=core
  [title [#Mixing a decimal literal with a float in arithmetic#]]
  [tags scalars decimal float arithmetic antipattern 698]
  [why [#
Almost every language reads a bare fraction as a binary float. CX does not:
`1.5` is a **decimal** — exact, base-10, arbitrary precision — and only an
`e`-exponent literal (`1.5e0`), a `::float` annotation, a `[cast … :float]`,
a division, `[$avg]`, or a float-returning `math` function produces a float.

The two kinds do not silently mix. Adding them is a loud refusal naming the
only bridge, rather than a quiet promotion that would put binary rounding
into a money column — which is precisely the class this rule exists to close.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[+ 1.5 1.0e0]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message='+: decimal/bigint arithmetic admits only int/bigint/decimal operands — [cast] is the only decimal↔float bridge (L44)']
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-decimal-float-mix-right level=core
  [title [#Keep exact quantities in decimals#]]
  [tags scalars decimal arithmetic 698]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $subtotal 19.99] [= $shipping 5.01] [total [+ $subtotal $shipping]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[total 25.00]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A7. an adapter lambda in front of an operator ─────────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-adapter-lambda-wrong level=core
  [title [#Wrapping an operator in a [?fn] to pass it somewhere#]]
  [tags callable operator-hole higher-order antipattern 1144 fe-1]
  [why [#
A `[?fn]` whose entire body is one application of one operator to its own
parameters is an ADAPTER — it exists only because the operator could not be
named as a value. It can be: a hole-bearing operator form IS the operator's
value (`[+ _ _]` binary, `[+ _ 1]` unary), accepted at every site a callable
goes — a `[using …]` clause, a higher-order std-lib parameter, an argument, a
map entry. Nothing stands between the operator and the slot, the arity is
visible in the holes, and the same value goes in an operator table
(`{'+': [+ _ _], '-': [- _ _]}`) that a runtime token can look up. The two
forms below are output-identical; the adapter is pure ceremony.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?reduce (1, 2, 3, 4) [using [?fn ($a $b) [+ $a $b]]] [init 0]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
10
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-adapter-lambda-right level=core
  [title [#The operator value itself#]]
  [tags callable operator-hole higher-order 1144 fe-1]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?reduce (1, 2, 3, 4) [using [+ _ _]] [init 0]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
10
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── A8. a hand-rolled else-arm around one real pattern ────────────── ]

 [case id=ap-match-else-rethrow-wrong level=core
  [title [#Multi-arm [?match] with one real arm and an else that re-raises#]]
  [tags match single-arm destructure antipattern 1144 fe-2]
  [why [#
This is the shape you write when you want "destructure it or fail", and the
only match form you know is the multi-arm one: one `[case]` doing the work,
one `[else]` inventing an error code for the shape that did not fit. The
single-arm form is exactly that, built in — `[?match SUBJECT PATTERN BODY]`
takes the full pattern grammar (sequence, array, map, element, rest `*$name`,
type tests) and a miss is the language's own loud NO_MATCH refusal, so the
bespoke error code and its wording stop being yours to maintain. One line,
and the pattern is still the whole contract. Reach for the multi-arm form
when you genuinely have SEVERAL shapes to dispatch between.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?match (3, 4, 9)
  [case ($b, $a, *$rest) [+ $a $b]]
  [else [err code='bad-stack' message='need at least two operands']]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
7
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-match-else-rethrow-right level=core
  [title [#Single-arm [?match] — destructure or refuse#]]
  [tags match single-arm destructure 1144 fe-2]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?match (3, 4, 9) ($b, $a, *$rest) [yield [+ $a $b]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
7
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-open-string-dispatch-wrong level=core
  [title [#Dispatching a closed set as bare strings, with [else] as the safety net#]]
  [tags match enum schema of antipattern 1154 en-1]
  [why [#
The set is closed in your head and open in the program. Nothing declares the
members, so nothing can check them: a misspelled arm never matches and never
complains, a member you forgot lands in `[else]`, and a value the upstream
producer added last week is indistinguishable from one you decided to ignore.
The `[else]` arm is doing two unrelated jobs at once — handling the cases you
chose not to enumerate, and swallowing the ones you did not know about.

Below, `:pending` is a real member the author simply forgot. It is reported as
`unknown`, at exit 0, forever. Declare the set in a schema and say what the
scrutinee ranges over (`[of TypeName]`): `cx lint` then names the missing
member, an out-of-set value refuses loudly at the boundary, and `[else]`
returns to meaning only "I deliberately do not enumerate the rest".
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?match 'pending'
  [case 'ok'  'done']
  [case 'err' 'failed']
  [else 'unknown']]
#]]
  [out-text [#
unknown
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-open-string-dispatch-right level=core
  [title [#[?match … [of TypeName]] — the closed set declared where it travels#]]
  [tags match enum schema of 1154 en-1]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?cx schema-inline
  [schema of=job]
  [type status::atom [enum :ok :err :pending]]]
[?match :pending [of status]
  [case :ok      'done']
  [case :err     'failed']
  [case :pending 'waiting']]
#]]
  [out-text [#
waiting
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]
]
